"ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-southeast-asia-taiwan/)\nciteturn1101view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1099view5\",\"id\":16}); Total lines: 154\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 23, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan\nL17: \nL18: ChatGPT Ads expands to seven additional markets across Asia and is now available in more than 60 countries.\nL19: \nL20: cite4†Get started with ChatGPT Ads(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: \nL26: ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing\nL27: \nL28:   * cite5†ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing L29:   * cite6†Guided by our ads principles L30:   * cite7†Building a global ads platform L31:   * cite5†ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing L32:   * cite6†Guided by our ads principles L33:   * cite7†Building a global ads platform L34: \nL35: Starting today, ChatGPT Ads will begin rolling out across Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam, and Taiwan. This announcement follows Asia Pacific launches in Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea and India, expanding access for businesses of all sizes across the region.\nL36: Advertisers can access ChatGPT Ads through the OpenAI Ads Solutions team, agency partners including dentsu, Havas Media, Omnicom Media, Publicis Groupe and WPP, and technology partners. Self-service access through cite4†Ads Manager⁠(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com is also available for eligible businesses.\nL37: Our mission is to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity, and Ads help democratize access to intelligence by supporting free and low-cost access to ChatGPT. As in our existing markets, ads will be shown only to users on the Free and Go plans. Plus, Pro, and Enterprise subscriptions will remain ad-free.\nL38: > “We’re at the beginning of a new chapter for advertising, with AI creating new ways for businesses and people to connect. Our expansion into more markets across Asia brings that opportunity to businesses of every size, from global brands looking for new ways to grow to entrepreneurs and startups running their first campaign.”\nL39: \nL40: —Dave Dugan, Vice President and Head of Global Ads Solutions, OpenAI\nL41: ## ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing\nL42: \nL43: People come to ChatGPT with goals. They might be planning a trip, choosing software for their business, or furnishing a home. Through conversation, people explain what they want to accomplish, what matters to them, and the constraints they face.\nL44: ChatGPT Ads give marketers an opportunity to reach people as they explore and evaluate their options. By introducing relevant products and services, ads can be useful in the moments that spur discovery and shape decisions.\nL45: > “OpenAI is a valued strategic partner for Shopee as we explore how AI can enhance the e-commerce experience for buyers while supporting sellers in growing their businesses. Across our markets, we are expanding access to AI-powered tools that help buyers discover what they need and enable sellers to reach and serve customers more effectively.\nL46: Following the launch of ChatGPT Ads in Brazil, we are pleased to extend this collaboration to Southeast Asia and Taiwan, marking another step in our deepening partnership with OpenAI.”\nL47: —Peggy Zhu, Executive Director, Brand and Growth Marketing, Shopee\nL48: ## Guided by our ads principles\nL49: \nL50: Protecting the trust people place in ChatGPT is foundational to our approach to advertising.\nL51: \nL52: Guided by OpenAI’s cite8†ads principles , we keep conversations private from advertisers and never sell customer data. Ads in ChatGPT are always clearly labeled and separate from ChatGPT’s answers, and advertising does not influence the answers ChatGPT provides. People also have control over ad personalization, with ad-free paid plans available for those who prefer not to see ads.\nL53: Our goal is straightforward: ads should be useful to people as they pursue their goals and make decisions, while also being effective for the businesses that advertise with us.\nL54: ## Building a global ads platform\nL55: At the end of cite9†August , we announced that ChatGPT Ads had reached $1 billion in annualized revenue run rate in less than 200 days after launch. Tens of thousands of advertisers are now running ads in ChatGPT, with many reaching people across multiple countries. This expansion brings that opportunity to more businesses across Southeast Asia and Taiwan. With today’s announcement, ChatGPT Ads are now available in over cite10†60 countries⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com .\nL56: Our progress to date represents only the first chapter in what ChatGPT Ads will become. Over the next few months, we will continue to expand into new markets, build new formats, optimization tools, and measurement solutions. Throughout this work, we remain focused on broadening access to AI, protecting the trust people place in ChatGPT, delivering useful ads to people, and helping businesses achieve their goals.\nL57: Businesses interested in getting started can visit cite4†ads.openai.com⁠(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com .\nL58:   * cite11†2026 L59:   * cite12†ChatGPT L60: ## Author\nL61: \nL62: OpenAI\nL63: ## Keep reading\nL64: \nL65: cite13†View all L66: \nL67: cite14†Better prompt caching for GPT-6 ProductSep 22, 2026 L68: \nL69: cite15†Introducing GPT-6 Sol and Luna ProductSep 22, 2026 L70: \nL71: cite16†Reimagining advertising with AI ProductSep 16, 2026 L72: \nL73: Research\nL74: \nL75:   * cite17†Research Index L76:   * cite18†Research Overview L77:   * cite19†Economic Research L78: \nL79: Latest Advancements\nL80: \nL81:   * cite20†GPT-6 L82:   * cite21†GPT-5.6 L83:   * cite22†GPT-5.5 L84:   * cite23†GPT-5.4 L85: \nL86: Safety\nL87:   * cite24†Safety Approach L88:   * cite25†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L89:   * cite26†Security & Privacy L90:   * cite27†Trust & Transparency L91: \nL92: Products\nL93: \nL94:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L95:   * cite28†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L96:   * cite29†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L97:   * cite30†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L98:   * cite31†Codex L99:   * cite32†Release Notes L100: \nL101: API Platform\nL102:   * cite33†Overview L103:   * cite34†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L104:   * cite35†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L105: \nL106: Business\nL107: \nL108:   * cite36†Overview L109:   * cite37†Solutions L110:   * cite38†Resources L111:   * cite39†Plugins L112:   * cite40†Customer Stories L113:   * cite41†Partner Network L114:   * cite42†Contact Sales L115: \nL116: Developers\nL117:   * cite43†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L118:   * cite44†Open Models L119:   * cite45†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L120:   * cite46†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L121:   * cite47†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L122: \nL123: Company\nL124: \nL125:   * cite48†About Us L126:   * cite49†Our Charter L127:   * cite50†Careers L128:   * cite13†News L129: \nL130: Support\nL131: \nL132:   * cite51†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L133: \nL134: More\nL135:   * cite52†Stories L136:   * cite53†Academy L137:   * cite54†Supply Co. L138:   * cite55†Livestreams L139:   * cite56†Podcast L140:   * cite57†RSS L141: \nL142: Terms & Policies\nL143: \nL144:   * cite58†Terms of Use L145:   * cite59†Privacy Policy L146:   * cite60†Other Policies L147: \nL148: cite61†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L149: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL150: \nL151: English United States\nL152: \nL153: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAirbnb widens access to GPT-6 Astra and OpenAI frontier models | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/airbnb-gpt-6-astra/)\nciteturn1101view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1099view5\",\"id\":17}); Total lines: 142\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 23, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # Airbnb widens access to GPT‑6 Astra and OpenAI frontier models\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Under a new agreement, Airbnb is giving its engineering and product development teams broader access to OpenAI frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra.\nL23: \nL24: The expansion builds on Airbnb’s use of Codex and longstanding collaboration with OpenAI. Airbnb engineers use an internal AI assistant for writing software and creating remote AI agents powered by Codex and models like GPT‑5.6 Sol, Terra, and Luna.\nL25: The new agreement gives Airbnb broader access to OpenAI models through OpenAI APIs and Amazon Bedrock. It arrives amid accelerating adoption of GPT‑6 Astra among developers, builders, and enterprises, many of which were already benefiting from the improved cite4†price-performance and efficiency of GPT‑5.6 Terra and Luna.\nL26: Airbnb’s early use of GPT‑6 Astra has shown strong results beyond coding. Astra helps Airbnb engineers accelerate their work by tracking down hard bugs, shaping system designs, and brainstorming engineering approaches. In one test involving strategic documents and other non-coding work, one Airbnb user reported reaching impressive output in 3–4 passes, compared with 20+ rounds using other models.\nL27: > “Our development teams are shipping roughly 80% more features than a year ago. OpenAI’s frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra, are a key element of our developer tooling that helps us keep that momentum going and build better products faster.”\nL28: \nL29: —Ahmad Al-Dahle, Chief Technology Officer at Airbnb\nL30: > “Airbnb has been using AI to rethink how software gets built, and expanding access to models like GPT‑6 Astra gives its teams even more powerful tools to move from ideas to production faster. We’re proud to deepen our work together as Airbnb puts OpenAI capabilities to work across engineering and the experiences it builds for millions of guests and hosts.”\nL31: \nL32: —Dali Rajic, Chief Revenue Officer at OpenAI\nL33: \nL34: 1 of 2\nL35: > “Our development teams are shipping roughly 80% more features than a year ago. OpenAI’s frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra, are a key element of our developer tooling that helps us keep that momentum going and build better products faster.”\nL36: \nL37: —Ahmad Al-Dahle, Chief Technology Officer at Airbnb\nL38: > “Airbnb has been using AI to rethink how software gets built, and expanding access to models like GPT‑6 Astra gives its teams even more powerful tools to move from ideas to production faster. We’re proud to deepen our work together as Airbnb puts OpenAI capabilities to work across engineering and the experiences it builds for millions of guests and hosts.”\nL39: \nL40: —Dali Rajic, Chief Revenue Officer at OpenAI\nL41: \nL42:   * Ahmad Al-Dahle\nL43:   * Dali Rajic\nL44: Airbnb also uses OpenAI models to help support experiences across search, fraud prevention, guest and host support, and insurance claims. This deep use of AI and machine learning across its marketplace helps the company better understand what guests are looking for, identify potentially fraudulent content or claims, help guests when travel plans change, and ensure hosts get insurance payouts faster and easier.\nL45: As Airbnb brings more of the trip into one app—across homes, services, experiences, airport pickups, car rentals, and more—OpenAI frontier models can give the teams building those features more powerful tools to move faster and explore what comes next.\nL46: \nL47:   * cite5†2026 L48: ## Author\nL49: \nL50: OpenAI\nL51: ## Keep reading\nL52: \nL53: cite6†View all L54: \nL55: cite7†Two years of OpenAI Academy CompanySep 23, 2026 L56: \nL57: cite8†Advisory group on mathematics and artificial intelligence CompanySep 21, 2026 L58: \nL59: cite9†Expanding OpenAI Academy with new learning paths CompanySep 21, 2026 L60: \nL61: Research\nL62: \nL63:   * cite10†Research Index L64:   * cite11†Research Overview L65:   * cite12†Economic Research L66: \nL67: Latest Advancements\nL68: \nL69:   * cite13†GPT-6 L70:   * cite14†GPT-5.6 L71:   * cite15†GPT-5.5 L72:   * cite16†GPT-5.4 L73: \nL74: Safety\nL75:   * cite17†Safety Approach L76:   * cite18†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L77:   * cite19†Security & Privacy L78:   * cite20†Trust & Transparency L79: \nL80: Products\nL81: \nL82:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L83:   * cite21†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L84:   * cite22†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L85:   * cite23†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L86:   * cite24†Codex L87:   * cite25†Release Notes L88: \nL89: API Platform\nL90:   * cite26†Overview L91:   * cite27†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L92:   * cite28†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L93: \nL94: Business\nL95: \nL96:   * cite29†Overview L97:   * cite30†Solutions L98:   * cite31†Resources L99:   * cite32†Plugins L100:   * cite33†Customer Stories L101:   * cite34†Partner Network L102:   * cite35†Contact Sales L103: \nL104: Developers\nL105:   * cite36†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L106:   * cite37†Open Models L107:   * cite38†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L108:   * cite39†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L109:   * cite40†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L110: \nL111: Company\nL112: \nL113:   * cite41†About Us L114:   * cite42†Our Charter L115:   * cite43†Careers L116:   * cite6†News L117: \nL118: Support\nL119: \nL120:   * cite44†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L121: \nL122: More\nL123:   * cite45†Stories L124:   * cite46†Academy L125:   * cite47†Supply Co. L126:   * cite48†Livestreams L127:   * cite49†Podcast L128:   * cite50†RSS L129: \nL130: Terms & Policies\nL131: \nL132:   * cite51†Terms of Use L133:   * cite52†Privacy Policy L134:   * cite53†Other Policies L135: \nL136: cite54†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite55†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite56†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite57†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite58†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L137: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL138: \nL139: English United States\nL140: \nL141: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-closedquorum-windows-malware-uses-ai-for-attack-decisions/)\nciteturn1101view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1099view4\",\"id\":3}); Total lines: 116\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # New ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Bill Toulas L8: \nL9:   * September 22, 2026\nL10:   * 02:04 PM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: New ClosedQuorum Windows malware uses AI for attack decisions†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: A new Windows malware named ClosedQuorum uses Google Gemini, DeepSeek, Qwen, and Mistral AI models to autonomously determine the actions to take during post-compromise stages of an attack.\nL16: The Go-based malware acts with no commands from a human operator, using reconnaissance information and a voting system to decide its next step on infected hosts.\nL17: \nL18: When votes are tied, DeepSeek has priority at making the final call, based on the option it considers most appropriate, followed by Qwen, Mistral, and Gemini.\nL19: \nL20: Cisco Talos researchers analyzing ClosedQuorum say that the models are restricted to a predefined set of decisions, including:\nL21:   * steal — simultaneously runs LSASS credential dumping, browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox) credential theft, and cryptocurrency-wallet (MetaMast, Exodus, Ethereum) extraction\nL22:   * inject — generates shellcode, then uses either process hollowing or Early Bird APC injection\nL23:   * persist — executes the malware’s persistence module\nL24:   * move — included as a possible lateral movement decision, but the analyzed distribution build has no corresponding handler, so it cannot execute this action\nL25: The stolen details are then passed to the operators via a Discord webhook, so apart from the malware delivery, the attack can be fully automated.\nL26: \nL27: cite3†Image: ClosedQuorum attack overview†www.bleepstatic.com ClosedQuorum attack model overview\nL28: Source: Cisco\nL29: \nL30: Talos describes ClosedQuorum as the first publicly documented Windows implant to delegate tactical command-and-control (C2) decisions to a panel of AI models, commenting that this adds greater speed and scaling potential to malicious operations.\nL31: The researchers also highlight that this eliminates human interaction, allowing the attack chain to proceed at any time.\nL32: \nL33: However, Cisco notes that this may also pose challenges in certain cases where rate limits are hit, output is malformed, or commercial APIs that the system relies on are temporarily unavailable.\nL34: Since it is not a sophisticated piece of malware, it is unclear if ClosedQuorum is a test or experiment. However, the researchers warn that it represents an \"architectural shift towards attack-chain automation.\"\nL35: \nL36: “While we do not have confirmation of in-the-wild deployment, artifacts from the binary were used to connect the developer to postings on criminal forums related to carding, dating back to 2025,” cite4†explains Cisco Talos†blog.talosintelligence.com .\nL37: The binary that Cisco Talos analyzed contains placeholder API credentials and a dummy Discord webhook, although the creators can add those in their custom ClosedQuorum builds.\nL38: \nL39: Cisco Talos discovered ClosedQuorum through cite5†CAIRN†blog.talosintelligence.com , an cite6†open-source toolkit†github.com it released to help researchers track and analyze AI-integrated malware.\nL40: ## cite7†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L41: \nL42: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL43: \nL44: cite7†Save your seat†hubs.li L45: \nL46:   * cite8†AI L47:   * cite9†AI Malware L48:   * cite10†Artificial Intelligence L49:   * cite11†ClosedQuorum L50:   * cite12†Malware L51:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Bill Toulas bill.toulas@bleepingcomputer.com\nL52: \nL53: Bill Toulas is a tech writer and infosec news reporter with over a decade of experience working on various online publications, covering open-source, Linux, malware, data breach incidents, and hacks.\nL54: \nL55:   * cite13†Previous Article L56:   * cite14†Next Article L57: ##### Post a Comment cite15†Community Rules L58: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL59: \nL60: [Input]\nL61: \nL62: Not a member yet? cite16†Register Now L63: \nL64:   Upcoming Webinar\nL65: \nL66: Popular Stories\nL67: \nL68:   * cite17†Researchers escape OpenAI Codex sandbox to run commands on host L69:   * cite18†Malicious npm packages evade install-script defenses at runtime L70:   * cite14†ShinyHunters claims FBI hack, data theft in PeopleSoft zero-day breach L71: \nL72: S ponsor Posts\nL73:   * cite19†Automate Onboarding and Access Reviews with No-Code IGA: See how it works†www.tenfold-security.com L74:   * cite20†AI is a data-breach time bomb: Read the new report†info.varonis.com L75:   * cite21†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.com L76:   * cite22†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.com L77:   * cite23†Build cyber resilience with Wazuh: The open-source SIEM & XDR for proactive protection†wazuh.com L78: ##### Follow us:\nL79: \nL80: ##### Main Sections\nL81: \nL82:   * cite24†News L83:   * cite25†Webinars L84:   * cite26†VPN Buyer Guides L85:   * cite27†SysAdmin Software Guides L86:   * cite28†Downloads L87:   * cite29†Virus Removal Guides L88:   * cite30†Tutorials L89:   * cite31†Startup Database L90:   * cite32†Uninstall Database L91:   * cite33†Glossary L92: \nL93: ##### Community\nL94: \nL95:   * cite34†Forums L96:   * cite35†Forum Rules L97:   * cite36†Chat L98: \nL99: ##### Useful Resources\nL100: \nL101:   * cite37†Welcome Guide L102:   * cite38†Sitemap L103: ##### Company\nL104: \nL105:   * cite39†About BleepingComputer L106:   * cite40†Contact Us L107:   * cite41†Send us a Tip! L108:   * cite42†Advertising L109:   * cite43†Write for BleepingComputer L110:   * cite44†Social & Feeds L111:   * cite45†Changelog L112: \nL113: cite46†Terms of Use - cite47†Privacy Policy - cite48†Ethics Statement - cite49†Affiliate Disclosure L114: \nL115: Copyright @ 2003 - 2026 cite24†Bleeping Computer^{®} LLC - All Rights Reserved\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClaude discovers a novel enzyme system \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-discovers-novel-enzyme-system)\nciteturn1101view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1099view6\",\"id\":12}); Total lines: 74\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10: Science\nL11: # Claude discovers a novel enzyme system with CRISPR-like repeats\nL12: \nL13: Sep 23, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite5†Image: Video thumbnail L16: We’re introducing a new life sciences research group and laboratory at Anthropic. Our focus is on fundamental biology research using Claude: exploring datasets of DNA to identify uncharacterized protein families, generating hypotheses at scale, and testing them through experiments in the lab. This post introduces the team behind this work and shares early results in which Claude discovered a novel enzyme system with properties reminiscent of CRISPR, with only high-level direction from our scientists.\nL17: Many discoveries that have revolutionized biology and medicine started with a scientist noticing something odd in the staggering diversity of molecular machines found in nature. Restriction enzymes, proteins that cut DNA at specific short sequences, were found in bacterial immune systems, where they destroy the DNA of invading viruses. Researchers realized they could use these enzymes to cut DNA at chosen places and splice genes from one organism into another, which launched the biotechnology industry.\nL18: Taq polymerase, an enzyme that copies DNA at high temperatures, was identified in a bacterium in a Yellowstone hotspring. It became the basis for PCR, the DNA-copying method used in much of modern diagnostics. CRISPR was first noticed as an unusual repeat sequence in the DNA of certain bacteria, and is now the foundation of gene editing-based medicines.\nL19: In the spring of 2026, we formed a research group to see whether general AI models can systematize and cite6†accelerate such discoveries†darioamodei.com . We believe that this acceleration will come from establishing a new way of doing biology research, in which agents collaborate with humans in every step of the process. Developing this new way of working required that we build our own lab and a single team working on everything from training Claude in biology to running experiments in the lab.\nL20: Today, we’re sharing early results from one of our first research programs, in which Claude autonomously discovered a novel enzyme system that is associated with an array of DNA repeats, a pattern reminiscent of CRISPR. Although we don’t yet know its function, the system that Claude discovered has a set of characteristics that have only ever been found together in a handful of other systems, all of which are programmable and perform operations like cutting, copying, and pasting DNA.\nL21: Beyond CRISPR, which has already transformed science and medicine, several other such systems are now in development as promising tools.\nL22: The system that Claude found is based on a reverse transcriptase (RT), enzymes that copy RNA into DNA. While this underlying RT, found in a jumbo phage, had been identified in previous studies, Claude appears to be the first to notice the system’s defining features—an associated array of non-coding DNA sequences and an additional accessory protein of unknown function.\nL23: After reviewing the pre-print, Feng Zhang, one of the pioneers of CRISPR genome editing and a professor at MIT and the Broad Institute said:\nL24: \nL25: > This is an exciting example of how AI agents can contribute to biological discovery. The identification of RNA-repeat arrays associated with reverse transcriptases is genuinely intriguing and merits further investigation. I hope this work encourages more scientists to explore how AI can support their research.\nL26: We gave Claude a prompt to search through a massive database of DNA sequences for interesting new examples of RTs. Our involvement was limited to the initial prompt and the lab work, while Claude agents combed through the database, investigated the distinct RT families, and used their own judgement to identify interesting candidates.\nL27: After 21 hours spent searching this data by roughly 950 agents using 210 million tokens, one of the agents spotted something remarkable: a repeating pattern of DNA sequences that occurs next to the gene for an odd-looking RT. After further analysis and testing in our lab, we recognized that this pattern marked a previously uncharacterized enzyme system found in bacteriophages (the viruses that infect bacteria) that we call array-associated reverse transcriptases (ART).\nL28: Our work to understand the primary function of ARTs is ongoing. However, we think it is important to share such findings early, both to demonstrate Claude’s capabilities and to give the broader community insight into what we’re working on. We have released a pre-print (cite7†here†www-cdn.anthropic.com ) that discusses this in more detail.\nL29: ## About our lab\nL30: We are a team of scientists who have spent our careers exploring unusual proteins, and specialize in using computational approaches to systematically read DNA, interpret its evolution, and pick out biological systems for further characterization.\nL31: Our research prior to joining Anthropic has helped to better understand the cite8†evolution†www.science.org and cite9†regulation†www.nature.com of CRISPR systems, discover new cite10†enzymes†www.nature.com for next-generation cite11†cell and gene therapies†www.science.org , and build cite12†tools†www.nature.com for accelerating the identification of anomalies in DNA, such as human pathogenic variants.\nL32: We are part of Anthropic’s life sciences organization, alongside teams whose work includes drug discovery, and training Claude in biology and chemistry.\nL33: Our lab, located in the Bay Area, looks like a typical molecular biology lab. We do research that involves only the lower-levels of the biosafety risk level (BSL-1 and BSL-2) and we do not handle pathogens that can infect humans. All of the lab work is performed by human scientists.\nL34: Although we’ve experimented with using AI to accelerate lab work with initiatives like the cite13†Model Hardware Standard , this approach is less conducive to the sort of ad hoc workflows that are involved in our molecular biology research.\nL35: ## How we work\nL36: Many of our workflows involve having Claude search through the vast collection of DNA sequences associated with proteins without a known function. One typical pattern begins with a survey of a given protein family. Claude reads the relevant literature and reproduces the established results from public data to check its methods.\nL37: It then searches for family members or genomic neighbors that fit no described system, and writes a short, human-readable report for each candidate that proposes a function and describes the evidence supporting its claims. In follow-up analyses, Claude critically evaluates the evidence—typically most candidates are eliminated at this stage. A survey may end with a single candidate worth testing, or with none.\nL38: When a candidate survives our review, we test it in the laboratory, expressing the protein in standard laboratory strains and characterizing it biochemically and structurally, with Claude helping to interpret the data. We do our work in cite14†Claude Science†claude.com and cite15†Claude Code†claude.com , the same tools available to any scientist, and sometimes with a harness of our own that coordinates many Claude sessions running in parallel.\nL39: Because Claude produces hypotheses so prolifically, the hypotheses themselves have become an object of study for us. With hundreds to thousands of candidate reports from a single campaign, we have been asking what distinguishes the proposals we judge worth testing from those we set aside. What we learn goes back into the instructions we give Claude and teaches it to mimic our own scientific taste.\nL40: ## Claude finds ART\nL41: \n"
